\section{Policies}

\subsection{Citing Sources}

In EE474, we expect that you will cite the source for
any information you borrow, quote, paraphrase, copy or otherwise use directly.
{\bf It is an instance of academic misconduct if you fail to do this.}

The UW College of Engineering establishes the procedures and penalties for
Academic Misconduct:

See \href{https://www.engr.washington.edu/current/policies/academic-integrity-misconduct}
    {College of Engineering Academic Misconduct Policy}
      for more information.

I will follow these procedures to the letter if any cases arise
in this class.

Purdue University has a useful tutorial how to understand and avoid plagiarism.

\href{https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/avoiding_plagiarism/index.html}{[ Purdue's plagarism page ]}.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\subsection{AI Policies:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item AI may be used for any assignment.
    \item Every code function must contain a 3-digit ``AI-acknowlegement''  in its header comment as
    described below.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{AI acknowledgement coding: }
The ``474 AI code'' consisists of the name of your chosen AI tool (e.g. ChatGPT, Claude Code, etc) followed by 3 single digits. Each digit represents
a percent of AI effort.  For example if AI did 90-100\% of the work,
code it as "9", 50\% as "5", etc.   The three digits stand for the three
phases:  Writing, Testing or bug detection, and Debugging.   Of course this only needs to be approximate
and it does it does not affect your grade (unless you leave it off).

<*>


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

If the effort is:\vspace{0.2in}

\begin{tabular}{c|c}
AI work  & digit code\\\hline
0-10\% AI  & 0\\
11-20\% AI & 1\\
\dots &\dots\\
90-100\% AI & 9
\end{tabular}\vspace{0.2in}

Examples:\vspace{0.2in}

\begin{tabular}{l|c|c|c|l}
Agent Name & Writing & Testing & Debugging & Description \\\hline
Claude.ai& 9&9&9 & AI did everything! \\
ChatGPT  & 0&0&0 & No AI was used at all\\
CoPilot  & 5&5&5 & approximately 50/50 AI + human\\
\end{tabular}

\vspace{0.2in}
Format your code as a compact string like:  {\bf Claude-436}
\vspace{0.2in}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


<*hnc>
{\bf Example: }   Yuhui coded a real-time task for a 474 Lab.  She dumped a paragraph from the assignment into
ChatGPT but didn't like the result so she wrote her own prompt.  Then she changed 2 out of 10 lines
to improve the generated code. So AI wrote 8/10 lines (score 8), but Yuhui gets some credit for writing an
effective prompt.  Let's score this one a 6.  Because the code function was to read and process data from a wired up sensor on the ESP32, it could only be tested in actual hardware so she did all the testing (``0'' for the Testing category).  While trying to debug the code, she didn't understand an error message so she pasted it into GPT along with the code.  GPT figured out the bug from these and AI fixed the code which then worked. Maybe this is a ``6''.


Therefore her AI-acknowledgment for this module is {\tt ``ChatGPT-606''}.


\subsection{Policies specific our assignments:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Exams in class:\\
      No cooperation, communication, or unauthorized materials.
\item  Exams will be closed book, and closed notes.
\item  You will be able to bring a hand written ``cheat sheet" of notes
to the exam.  The cheat sheet may not contain photocopied material.
\item Lab assignments:\\
      No copying, dictation, or file sharing between lab groups. Any code or
      text included in projects and reports from other sources must have complete
      and clear indication of the source of this code or text.
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\section{ Grading System, Spring 26}
\begin{itemize}
        \item ``Flat'' point system.
        \item 450 total points.
        \item Point breakdown is given in the third tab of the 
        \href{https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/16EKCsbianN9MxyGyvhjx5A7XP5cWTcK8c6I8fwjFqso/edit?gid=870260692#gid=870260692}
        {Course Syllabus Sheet} 

        \item Grade will be obtained by dividing your total by 100.
        \item Team members receive same Lab grade (except lab 1).
        \item Each lab's grade is computed based on Demo, Code, and Report.
%         \item At end of term, students will rate the lab performance of each team-mate.
        \item In exceptional cases a student's lab grade may be adjusted
              up or down relative to their teammates.
        \item Individual final grades still differ from your lab mate because of
        individual performance on C-coding assignments, quizes, and exams.\vspace{0.35in}
        
        \item {\bf Note: } At end of term, at the discretion of the instructor, the class mean
        may be adjusted by adding {\bf or} subtracting an offset from all students in
              the class.  The purpose of this uniform offset adjustment is to ensure
              that the class average properly reflects overall class learning, overcoming
              of special difficulties, and similar factors.
\end{itemize}

  
